{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>You shall not go about as a talebearer, etc.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory preceded [me] in the holy Zohar and examined the doubling of the terms — <b>תֵלֵךְ</b> (go), <b>רָכִיל</b> (talebearer) — for they are one [prohibition]. And they spoke by way of sod; one may investigate their words there in the Idra (Zohar Naso 128). And according to the plain sense of the verse, it appears that it intends to say that he should not cause the talebearing that is produced by others through his agency. How so? That he should not disclose matters even to someone who has no talebearing intent, in a situation where there is reason to suspect that the listeners will pass the matters along — and so it turns out that he is the one who carried this tale that the intermediaries spread. And according to this approach, the precision of saying <b>בְּעַמֶּךָ</b> (among your people) is well understood — meaning, through the medium of your people you carry the tale, for the matters are said in their presence and roll from this one to that one and the matters become heard. And it says <b>אֲנִי ה׳</b> — meaning, I examine the matters, from whom they originated at first, and I exact payment." "<b>Further,</b> it intends that one should not lend support to the hands of evil speakers, for in this he enables the carrying of the tale, and Scripture holds him accountable as though he himself carried the tale to that person, since he was the cause." "<b>Nor shall you stand, etc.</b> Because He commanded concerning talebearing, He stipulated regarding it that one may not stand idly by the blood of one's fellow: if he saw a band of killers intending to murder, he is obligated to inform the person involved so that he may save himself — and he should not say, 'this is talebearing.' From this you learn that if he did not reveal it to his fellow's ear and they killed him, he has violated the prohibition of ״לֹא תַעֲמֹד״ (you shall not stand idly by), etc. Go out and learn from the episode of Gedaliah son of Ahikam (Jeremiah 40:14), who was warned, and when he paid no heed — see what came of it."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:59:12.351337544Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}